 2. Q3 productivity surge 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Bureau of Labor Statistics; Chart: Neil Irwin/Axios Productivity growth was strong in the third quarter, the latest sign that America's growth potential looks bright.

 Driving the news: Output per hour of labor rose at a 3.6% annual rate in the July through September period, the Labor Department said this morning , the strongest in a year.

 It follows three softer quarters of productivity growth that had cast doubt on a post-pandemic surge in businesses continuing to find ways to create more output from a finite labor supply. Unit labor costs — how much businesses spend on labor for each unit of output they produce — fell at a 0.7% rate in Q3, a disinflationary sign that could reassure the Federal Reserve that price pressures are under control. Reality check: Quarterly productivity data is volatile and can offer more noise than signal. Longer time horizons give a more reliable signal.

 Over the last three years, labor productivity has risen at a 2.6% annual rate. It was higher than that in the 2022-2025 period, but the recent results easily surpass the roughly 1% annual productivity gains seen in the 2010s. That should allow for stronger gains in growth and incomes over time, even as demographic change constrains the supply of labor. The intrigue: The rising use of AI may be part of the productivity story, but given the surge started years ago, there's reason to think that it has more to do — for now, at least — with changes in work processes, more intensive use of capital and a burst of entrepreneurship in recent years.

 Richard de Chazal with William Blair wrote in a note that there is "still-limited evidence that this represents a broad AI-driven productivity boom." "Rather, we see much of the more recent improvement as being the result of post-COVID restructuring, efficiency gains, and capital deepening, as well as some early AI-related benefits," he added. Of note: In its recent policy statements, the policy-setting Federal Open Market Committee has said that productivity growth is "strong," but that didn't really align with the numbers in late 2025 and the first half of 2026.

 The strong third-quarter result puts the data more closely in line with the Fed's description of the state of affairs. 
 
